  "featuredImage": "/images/services/tub-to-shower-conversion.jpg",
  "metaTitle": "Tub-to-Shower Drain Size: Arizona Plumbing Code | ARZ",
  "metaDescription": "Do you need a 2-inch drain to convert a tub to a shower in Arizona? Read the UPC code requirements, costs, slab cutting, and plumbing rules.",
  "author": "Alex Rivers",
  "status": "published",
  "publishedAt": "2026-07-20T09:00:00Z",
  "createdAt": "2026-07-20T09:00:00Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-07-20T09:00:00Z",
  "content": "\n# Tub-to-Shower Conversion: Arizona Plumbing Code Drain Requirements\n\nConverting an unused bathtub into a modern walk-in shower is one of the most popular home renovations in Chandler, Arizona. However, behind the beautiful porcelain tiles and frameless glass lies a critical technical requirement that many homeowners and unlicensed contractors overlook: **the shower drain size**.\n\nIf you are planning a tub-to-shower conversion, it is essential to understand the plumbing code requirements enforced by municipal inspectors in Chandler and the East Valley. In this guide, we explain why you must upgrade your drain line and how this process is executed safely.\n\n## The Code: Why Shower Drains Must Be Larger Than Tub Drains\n\nThe plumbing standards in Arizona are based on the **Uniform Plumbing Code (UPC)**. Under the UPC, bathtubs and showers have different drain size requirements:\n*   **Bathtubs (UPC Section 702.1):** Standard bathtubs are permitted to use a **1.5-inch drain line**. Because bathtubs hold water and are drained slowly, a smaller pipe is sufficient.\n*   **Showers (UPC Section 702.1):** Custom walk-in showers are required to have a minimum **2-inch drain line**. Because a shower is a continuous-flow fixture, a larger drain is needed to handle the high volume of water from modern showerheads and prevent overflow pooling.\n\n### The Risk of Failing to Upgrade\nIf a contractor attempts to connect a new custom tiled shower to your home's existing 1.5-inch tub drain line:\n1.  **Failing Inspections:** The City of Chandler Development Services inspectors will fail the rough-in plumbing inspection, halting your project.\n2.  **Drain Backup:** The smaller pipe will drain water slower than the showerhead outputs, causing soapy water to pool around your ankles and potentially overflow onto your bathroom floor.\n3.  **Voided Warranties:** Installing a custom shower system (like Schluter-KERDI) on an under-sized drain voids the manufacturer warranty.\n\n---\n\n## How ARZ Performs the Drain Upgrade (Concrete Slab Cutting)\n\nMost homes in Chandler are built on concrete slab-on-grade foundations. Upgrading a drain line in a slab home requires structural execution:\n1.  **Locating Post-Tension Cables:** Many East Valley slabs contain high-tension steel cables. Cutting one of these cables can compromise your home's structural integrity. We map the slab prior to cutting to ensure complete safety.\n2.  **Saw-Cutting and Trenching:** Using wet-cutting concrete saws with integrated dust extraction, we carefully cut a trench in the bathroom floor to access the original P-trap.\n3.  **Tapping into the Main Line:** We remove the 1.5-inch trap and tap into the 2-inch or 3-inch waste line further downstream.\n4.  **Re-pouring Concrete:** Once the new 2-inch PVC drain is installed and pressure-tested, we fill the trench and re-pour concrete to create a solid, level subfloor.\n\nFor more details on the pricing of this entire conversion process, check our [tub to shower conversion cost](/tub-to-shower-conversion-cost/) guide.\n\n---\n\n## Work with a Licensed Plumbing Specialist\n\nModifying foundation slabs and drain lines is a high-risk task that must be handled by licensed professionals. ARZ Home Remodeling is licensed under **#ROC338304** and specializes in technical shower conversions. We handle all permit filings and structural inspections through the City of Chandler.\n\nAre you ready to convert your unused tub into a spacious walk-in shower? Review our [tub-to-shower conversion Chandler AZ](/tub-to-shower-conversion/) services and call Mike Hernandez at **(520) 569-3339** to schedule your free, itemized estimate.\n"
